 and South Weld County","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.56f07ccd30017f7a70b6a91ef8df2e38a8814570.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.56f07ccd30017f7a70b6a91ef8df2e38a8814570.001.1"},{"event":"Air Quality Alert","headline":"Air Quality Alert issued August 27 at 4:10PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"The Colorado Department of Public Health and Environment has\nissued the following...\n\nWHAT...Ozone Action Day Alert.\n\nWHERE...Douglas, Jefferson, Denver, western Arapahoe, western\nAdams, Broomfield, Boulder, Larimer, and Weld Counties\n\nWHEN...400 PM Thursday August 27 to 400 PM Friday August 28\n\nIMPACTS...Ozone Action Day Alert is now in effect for the Front\nRange Urban Corridor.  Hot and sunny weather with scattered\nafternoon storms may allow ozone to reach the Unhealthy for\nSensitive Groups category in western portions of the Denver Metro\narea on Thursday and Friday. For Colorado air quality conditions,\nforecasts, and advisories, visit:\nhttps://www.colorado.gov/airquality/colorado_summary.aspx   If\npossible, please help us reduce ozone pollution by limiting\ndriving gas and diesel-powered vehicles until at least 4 p.m.\nFriday, August 28, 2026. Colorado is also addressing ozone\npollution through state policies. For more information about\nhealth impacts and how ozone pollution forms in Colorado, visit:\nhttps://cdphe.colorado.gov/ozone- pollution-and-your-health\n\nHEALTH INFORMATION...Increasing likelihood of respiratory symptoms\nand breathing discomfort in active children and adults and people\nwith lung disease, such as asthma. Active children and adults, and\npeople with lung disease, such as asthma, should reduce prolonged\nor heavy outdoor exertion.","ends":"2026-08-28T16:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Broomfield, CO; Jefferson, CO; Denver, CO; Douglas, CO; Adams, CO; Boulder, CO; Weld, CO; Larimer, CO; Arapahoe, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a17a27b506afd523954d57cd2deb7b4d9d4ce26d.001.1","url